# React and Next.js Best Practices

## Component Structure

### Functional Components
- Use functional components with TypeScript interfaces
- Prefer arrow functions for component definitions
- Use proper prop destructuring with default values

```typescript
// Good: Functional component with proper typing
interface OrderSummaryProps {
  order: Order;
  onCancel: () => void;
  isLoading?: boolean;
}

const OrderSummary: React.FC<OrderSummaryProps> = ({
  order,
  onCancel,
  isLoading = false
}) => {
  // Component implementation
};
```

### Server vs Client Components
- Prefer Server Components over Client Components
- Use `"use client"` directive only when necessary for interactivity
- Fetch data in Server Components and pass to Client Components

```typescript
// Good: Server Component for data fetching
async function OrdersPage() {
  const orders = await getOrders();
  
  return (
    <div>
      <OrdersList orders={orders} />
    </div>
  );
}

// Good: Client Component for interactivity
"use client";

interface OrdersListProps {
  orders: Order[];
}

const OrdersList: React.FC<OrdersListProps> = ({ orders }) => {
  const [filter, setFilter] = useState('all');
  
  return (
    // Interactive component implementation
  );
};
```

## State Management

### React Hooks Best Practices
- Use useState for local component state
- Use useEffect sparingly - prefer event handlers
- Use useCallback for memoizing event handlers
- Use useMemo for expensive calculations

```typescript
// Good: Proper hook usage
const MenuPage: React.FC = () => {
  const [selectedCategory, setSelectedCategory] = useState('all');
  const [cart, setCart] = useState<CartItem[]>([]);
  
  const handleAddToCart = useCallback((item: MenuItem) => {
    setCart(prev => [...prev, { ...item, quantity: 1 }]);
  }, []);
  
  const filteredItems = useMemo(() => {
    return items.filter(item => 
      selectedCategory === 'all' || item.category === selectedCategory
    );
  }, [items, selectedCategory]);
  
  return (
    // Component JSX
  );
};
```

### Context Usage
- Use React Context for global state
- Keep context providers close to where they're needed
- Use multiple contexts instead of one large context

```typescript
// Good: Specific context for cart
interface CartContextType {
  cart: CartItem[];
  addToCart: (item: MenuItem) => void;
  removeFromCart: (itemId: string) => void;
  clearCart: () => void;
}

const CartContext = createContext<CartContextType | undefined>(undefined);

export const useCart = () => {
  const context = useContext(CartContext);
  if (!context) {
    throw new Error('useCart must be used within CartProvider');
  }
  return context;
};
```

## Next.js App Router Best Practices

### File Organization
- Use the app directory structure
- Create page.tsx for pages
- Create layout.tsx for shared layouts
- Use loading.tsx for loading states
- Use error.tsx for error handling

```
app/
├── layout.tsx          # Root layout
├── page.tsx           # Home page
├── admin/
│   ├── layout.tsx     # Admin layout
│   ├── page.tsx       # Admin dashboard
│   └── orders/
│       ├── page.tsx   # Orders list
│       └── [id]/
│           └── page.tsx # Order details
└── student/
    ├── layout.tsx     # Student layout
    ├── page.tsx       # Student dashboard
    └── order/
        └── page.tsx   # Order form
```

### Data Fetching
- Use Server Components for data fetching
- Use React Suspense for loading states
- Implement proper error boundaries

```typescript
// Good: Server Component with data fetching
async function OrderDetailsPage({ params }: { params: { id: string } }) {
  const order = await getOrderById(params.id);
  
  if (!order) {
    notFound();
  }
  
  return (
    <Suspense fallback={<OrderDetailsSkeleton />}>
      <OrderDetails order={order} />
    </Suspense>
  );
}
```

### Server Actions
- Use Server Actions for form submissions
- Implement proper error handling
- Use redirect() for navigation after actions

```typescript
// Good: Server Action with proper error handling
async function createOrder(formData: FormData) {
  'use server';
  
  try {
    const orderData = {
      items: JSON.parse(formData.get('items') as string),
      deliveryDate: formData.get('deliveryDate') as string,
    };
    
    const order = await prisma.order.create({
      data: orderData,
    });
    
    revalidatePath('/student/orders');
    redirect(`/student/orders/${order.id}`);
  } catch (error) {
    return { error: 'Failed to create order' };
  }
}
```

## Error Handling

### Error Boundaries
- Implement error boundaries for unexpected errors
- Use error.tsx files for route-level error handling
- Provide fallback UI for errors

```typescript
// Good: Error boundary component
interface ErrorBoundaryProps {
  error: Error;
  reset: () => void;
}

const ErrorBoundary: React.FC<ErrorBoundaryProps> = ({ error, reset }) => {
  return (
    <div className="error-boundary">
      <h2>Something went wrong!</h2>
      <p>{error.message}</p>
      <button onClick={reset}>Try again</button>
    </div>
  );
};
```

### Loading States
- Use loading.tsx for page-level loading
- Implement skeleton components for better UX
- Use Suspense for component-level loading

```typescript
// Good: Loading component
const OrderListSkeleton: React.FC = () => {
  return (
    <div className="space-y-4">
      {[...Array(5)].map((_, i) => (
        <div key={i} className="animate-pulse">
          <div className="h-4 bg-gray-300 rounded w-3/4"></div>
          <div className="h-4 bg-gray-300 rounded w-1/2 mt-2"></div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
};
```

## Performance Optimization

### React.memo and Memoization
- Use React.memo for expensive components
- Use useMemo for expensive calculations
- Use useCallback for event handlers

```typescript
// Good: Memoized component
const MenuItemCard = React.memo<MenuItemCardProps>(({ item, onAddToCart }) => {
  return (
    <div className="menu-item-card">
      <h3>{item.name}</h3>
      <p>{item.description}</p>
      <button onClick={() => onAddToCart(item)}>
        Add to Cart
      </button>
    </div>
  );
});
```

### Code Splitting
- Use dynamic imports for code splitting
- Implement lazy loading for non-critical components
- Use Next.js built-in optimization features

```typescript
// Good: Dynamic import
const AdminDashboard = dynamic(() => import('./AdminDashboard'), {
  loading: () => <DashboardSkeleton />,
  ssr: false,
});
```

## Common Patterns to Avoid

### Anti-patterns
- Don't use useEffect for data fetching in Server Components
- Don't put business logic in components
- Don't forget to handle loading and error states
- Don't use inline styles - use Tailwind classes
- Don't mutate state directly - use immutable updates
- Don't use string refs - use useRef hook

### Best Practices
- Keep components small and focused
- Use composition over inheritance
- Implement proper prop validation
- Use consistent naming conventions
- Write self-documenting code
- Test components thoroughly
